WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.770 --> 00:00:04.930
More often than not, horror sequels are downgraded
ripoffs of the original, wear out what may

00:00:04.930 --> 00:00:09.090
have been a novel concept to begin with, or
resort to extreme gore and jump scares to

00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:10.080
one-up their predecessor.

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:11.560
"Stab 2?

00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:12.920
Who would want to do that?

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:13.920
Sequels suck."

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:15.110
"Oh, come on, man.”

00:00:15.110 --> 00:00:18.370
“Oh please, please, by definition alone
they're inferior films."

00:00:18.370 --> 00:00:22.630
That's true of most sequels, really, but it
seems especially common in this genre.

00:00:22.630 --> 00:00:27.430
There are exceptions to every rule, however—and
these sequels not only live up to their horrifying

00:00:27.430 --> 00:00:30.160
predecessors, they're even better.

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:31.470
Evil Dead II

00:00:31.470 --> 00:00:35.540
Without The Evil Dead, we would have never
been introduced to Ash and his cabin of horrors.

00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:39.050
So, there's certainly something to be said
for the merits of the movie that started it

00:00:39.050 --> 00:00:40.050
all.

00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:44.050
However, the "re-quel," as Bruce Campbell
calls it, was a lot campier and more self-aware—and,

00:00:44.050 --> 00:00:46.940
as a result, more fun.

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:48.980
"Groovy."

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:53.130
Instead of Ash coming in as the prototypical
hero type, he became a lot more humorous and

00:00:53.130 --> 00:00:55.020
quirky.

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:56.910
[Laughter]

00:00:56.910 --> 00:01:02.110
Sure, this meant the gore got dialed down
a bit, but it also set a new tone for the

00:01:02.110 --> 00:01:05.689
series that was much cheekier and far more
unique than the first.

00:01:05.689 --> 00:01:06.689
Evil Ash!

00:01:06.689 --> 00:01:07.700
"Groovy" Ash!

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:08.600
More mania!

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:11.060
[Scream]

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:17.240
Evil Dead II cemented the series as a viable
franchise, opening the door for movie three,

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:22.259
Army of Darkness, as well as a remake and
the TV spinoff series Ash vs. Evil Dead.

00:01:22.259 --> 00:01:25.590
Bruce Campbell may have done other work in
between all these Evil Dead installments,

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:29.529
but he'll still be forever be known for his
demonic slaughtering skills.

00:01:29.529 --> 00:01:30.529
"Yeah!

00:01:30.529 --> 00:01:31.649
Lookin good.

00:01:31.649 --> 00:01:33.429
Lookin sweet!"

00:01:33.429 --> 00:01:35.689
Friday the 13th, Part 2

00:01:35.689 --> 00:01:39.420
The first Friday the 13th certainly set a
tone for what would become a sprawling series

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:42.869
of slasher pics targeting teen counselors
at Camp Crystal Lake.

00:01:42.869 --> 00:01:45.759
"The counselors weren't paying any attention.

00:01:45.759 --> 00:01:48.909
They were making love while that young boy
drowned.

00:01:48.909 --> 00:01:51.110
His name was Jason."

00:01:51.110 --> 00:01:55.819
But its commitment to realism in having a
villain who's a real person, rather than future

00:01:55.819 --> 00:01:59.630
franchise mainstay Jason Voorhees, meant it
wasn't really all that different from any

00:01:59.630 --> 00:02:03.680
other entry in the "teens running from the
psycho" subgenre.

00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:06.340
"Must be my imagination."

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:10.940
The second movie, however, threw caution to
the wind and resurrected Jason from his watery

00:02:10.940 --> 00:02:12.540
grave to do all the gore-dealing.

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:14.750
And it totally paid off.

00:02:14.750 --> 00:02:17.410
"Here it comes!”

00:02:17.410 --> 00:02:18.410
[Laughter]

00:02:18.410 --> 00:02:22.370
Not only is Friday the 13th, Part 2 eerie
and well-made, but the idea that Jason would

00:02:22.370 --> 00:02:26.350
be the one to take up his mother's torch and
terrorize campers for the next few decades

00:02:26.350 --> 00:02:28.360
was solid and sustainable.

00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:31.160
"And he's hungry.

00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:33.960
Jason's out there."

00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:37.930
Without this movie, Jason Voorhees wouldn't
be such an icon of the horror genre, which

00:02:37.930 --> 00:02:40.040
is just plain unimaginable.

00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:41.680
The Devil's Rejects

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:46.600
Rob Zombie's House of 1000 Corpses was a shockingly
brutal parade of violence and mayhem, and

00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:50.120
produced some pretty unforgettable moments
of violence that will haunt many nightmares

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:51.230
forever.

00:02:51.230 --> 00:02:54.580
"Behold, fishboy!"

00:02:54.580 --> 00:02:56.820
"Oh my god!”

00:02:56.820 --> 00:03:01.290
But The Devil's Rejects came along and took
its trio of nefarious butchers into more realistically

00:03:01.290 --> 00:03:02.560
terrifying territory.

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:07.480
Baby, Otis, and Captain Spaulding are seen
fleeing the police and tormenting anyone unfortunate

00:03:07.480 --> 00:03:09.350
enough to find themselves in their path.

00:03:09.350 --> 00:03:12.850
"You're not gonna slay us are you?"

00:03:12.850 --> 00:03:15.300
"Ugh, slaying sounds so permanent."

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:19.430
The Devil's Rejects may have lost the atmospheric
creepiness of the original, but it somehow

00:03:19.430 --> 00:03:23.450
managed to be even more disturbing, since
all these slayings were done in plain sight.

00:03:23.450 --> 00:03:27.210
The depravity of these three was downright
horrifying, and the fact that the movie was

00:03:27.210 --> 00:03:33.210
so packed with relatable victims made the
dread that much more visceral than its predecessor.

00:03:33.210 --> 00:03:36.640
"What's the matter kid, don't you like clowns?"

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:38.720
Saw II

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:43.220
Saw is a modern horror classic that introduced
audiences to a game no one in their right

00:03:43.220 --> 00:03:44.511
mind would ever want to play.

00:03:44.511 --> 00:03:46.960
"I want to play a game.”

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:51.740
With special focus on the two victims of the
day, Saw presented Jigsaw and his philosophical

00:03:51.740 --> 00:03:55.340
torture-dealing scheme and paid off with a
twist ending that opened the dirty bathroom

00:03:55.340 --> 00:03:57.880
door to a whole franchise of gory traps.

00:03:57.880 --> 00:04:00.130
"Hello Dr. Gordon.

00:04:00.130 --> 00:04:04.630
You are perhaps my greatest asset."

00:04:04.630 --> 00:04:09.130
But where the first movie suffered some pacing
issues due to its shoestring budget and tight

00:04:09.130 --> 00:04:13.500
set space, the second movie cleverly expanded
the Jigsaw universe.

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:16.120
"Oh, yes.

00:04:16.120 --> 00:04:19.019
There will be blood."

00:04:19.019 --> 00:04:23.900
Not only did it present the first film's survivor
Amanda as Jigsaw's protége, but it also committed

00:04:23.900 --> 00:04:27.729
to its puzzle dynamic and got even more creative
with its terrifying traps.

00:04:27.729 --> 00:04:29.580
"Let the game begin."

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:34.090
Saw II was more exciting and interactive with
the audience, and it offered much more insight

00:04:34.090 --> 00:04:36.539
into who Jigsaw was and what he wanted to
accomplish.

00:04:36.539 --> 00:04:44.469
"By creating a legacy, by living a life worth
remembering, you become immortal."

00:04:44.469 --> 00:04:48.939
With the added layer of a bigger game in play
for one of the detectives investigating Jigsaw's

00:04:48.939 --> 00:04:53.210
crimes, Saw II began to really flesh out the
formula introduced by the first and offered

00:04:53.210 --> 00:04:56.930
a sustainable launch pad for the bevy of sequels
to follow.

00:04:56.930 --> 00:04:58.360
"Game over."

00:04:58.360 --> 00:04:59.930
Wes Craven's New Nightmare

00:04:59.930 --> 00:05:04.240
A Nightmare on Elm Street's original concept—that
a boogeyman who haunted dreams could actually

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:08.029
merk a person in their sleep—was enough
to make anyone afraid of bedtime.

00:05:08.029 --> 00:05:11.029
"This… is God."

00:05:11.029 --> 00:05:16.580
But once that storyline was beaten to lifelessness
with a slew of progressively lame sequels,

00:05:16.580 --> 00:05:20.210
franchise mastermind Wes Craven decided to
get a little more creative about how to amp

00:05:20.210 --> 00:05:21.220
up the intrigue.

00:05:21.220 --> 00:05:25.419
With Wes Craven's New Nightmare, he ventured
outside of the box and pegged all the predecessors

00:05:25.419 --> 00:05:27.550
as the fictional movies that they were.

00:05:27.550 --> 00:05:30.100
"I'm doing a film about my nightmares as I'm
dreaming them.

00:05:30.100 --> 00:05:33.939
In order for the movie to continue, it was
dependant on me having more nightmares.

00:05:33.939 --> 00:05:35.620
Well, fortunately, I did."

00:05:35.620 --> 00:05:40.069
Once his creation Freddy Krueger started bleeding
into real life, though, his original stars

00:05:40.069 --> 00:05:44.379
had to play their old parts in an effort to
stop the very evil he'd created with the original

00:05:44.379 --> 00:05:45.379
series.

00:05:45.379 --> 00:05:49.139
What resulted was a smart statement about
the industry—and his own place in it -- and

00:05:49.139 --> 00:05:53.610
a film that was both self-aware and completely
entertaining at the same time.

00:05:53.610 --> 00:05:58.210
"Nine, ten, never sleep again.”

00:05:58.210 --> 00:05:59.419
Scream 2

00:05:59.419 --> 00:06:03.699
Scream remains a central piece of '90s slasher
history thanks to its quotable psycho and

00:06:03.699 --> 00:06:05.210
fascination with its own genre.

00:06:05.210 --> 00:06:07.090
"Do you like scary movies?"

00:06:07.090 --> 00:06:08.090
"Uh huh."

00:06:08.090 --> 00:06:12.240
And its sequel, Scream 2, took the property
to new heights by focusing on what made the

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:15.130
original so, well, original.

00:06:15.130 --> 00:06:19.500
The first installment followed a fairly standard
formula about a masked psycho offing high

00:06:19.500 --> 00:06:23.589
school students, but there was an element
of satisfying self-awareness that set the

00:06:23.589 --> 00:06:24.589
movie apart.

00:06:24.589 --> 00:06:29.150
Scream 2 accentuated that aspect by introducing
a movie-within-a-movie storyline that winked

00:06:29.150 --> 00:06:31.720
at its own pop cultural relevance in a charming
new way.

00:06:31.720 --> 00:06:32.720
"See, I've got my whole defense planned out.

00:06:32.720 --> 00:06:38.389
I'm gonna blame the movies."

00:06:38.389 --> 00:06:41.789
While some of the slay scenes from the first
film were a little more thrilling than anything

00:06:41.789 --> 00:06:45.520
that came after, the first sequel's subtle
statements about itself were refreshingly

00:06:45.520 --> 00:06:46.990
on point.

00:06:46.990 --> 00:06:49.550
"It's showtime!"

00:06:49.550 --> 00:06:51.339
The Purge: Anarchy

00:06:51.339 --> 00:06:55.599
The Purge was laser focused on a single family
trying to survive America's annual night of

00:06:55.599 --> 00:06:57.300
government-sanctioned mayhem.

00:06:57.300 --> 00:07:01.430
"Please just let us purge."

00:07:01.430 --> 00:07:04.789
And while that was exciting enough to spawn
a sequel, there was a lot of room to grow

00:07:04.789 --> 00:07:08.089
once the concept proved to be viable at the
box office.

00:07:08.089 --> 00:07:12.259
Instead of just focusing again on a select
few struggling to survive the night, The Purge:

00:07:12.259 --> 00:07:16.379
Anarchy introduced some of the socioeconomic
factors that would be an important part in

00:07:16.379 --> 00:07:18.639
creating such a system of disorder.

00:07:18.639 --> 00:07:22.319
While fans of the first film might've been
left to wonder why and how the U.S. stooped

00:07:22.319 --> 00:07:26.289
to allowing such chaos, Anarchy attempted
to answer those questions.

00:07:26.289 --> 00:07:29.770
By putting its central players outside the
comfort of their own homes, we got to see

00:07:29.770 --> 00:07:33.779
a lot more of the destruction and fallout
from the Purge, and it was a lot more satisfying

00:07:33.779 --> 00:07:34.990
and informative.

00:07:34.990 --> 00:07:38.009
"Can't have heroes."

00:07:38.009 --> 00:07:41.969
The parallels to the occupy movement also
set the stage for the third installment, The

00:07:41.969 --> 00:07:46.349
Purge: Election Year, and the pic also left
room for the forthcoming prequel series that'll

00:07:46.349 --> 00:07:49.729
delve into how society devolved in the first
place.

00:07:49.729 --> 00:07:50.809
Dawn of the Dead

00:07:50.809 --> 00:07:54.030
George A. Romero's Night of the Living Dead
is a classic, no doubt about it.

00:07:54.030 --> 00:07:57.860
It marked the beginning of the zombie film
craze and holds up as an esteemed chapter

00:07:57.860 --> 00:07:59.340
in his filmography.

00:07:59.340 --> 00:08:03.069
"They're coming to get you Barbara."

00:08:03.069 --> 00:08:08.449
However, Dawn of the Dead, the second installment
in his Living Dead series, expanded the first

00:08:08.449 --> 00:08:11.879
movie's ideas by showcasing the root cause
of this epidemic.

00:08:11.879 --> 00:08:19.480
"When there’s no more room in Hell, the
dead will walk the earth."

00:08:19.480 --> 00:08:23.800
The film more narrowly pinpointed Romero's
parallels between zombification and American

00:08:23.800 --> 00:08:27.289
consumerism by having its victims take cover
in a shopping mall.

00:08:27.289 --> 00:08:31.509
And the film's juxtaposition of excess and
materialism with hopelessness and violence

00:08:31.509 --> 00:08:34.290
helped define the zombie genre as a whole
forever.

00:08:34.290 --> 00:08:36.710
The Texas Chainsaw Massacre: The Beginning

00:08:36.710 --> 00:08:41.090
When it comes to the Texas Chainsaw series,
nothing has or probably ever will beat Tobe

00:08:41.090 --> 00:08:43.200
Hooper's 1974 original.

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:46.350
When it comes to the modern reboots, though,
there are some which were better than the

00:08:46.350 --> 00:08:47.350
others.

00:08:47.350 --> 00:08:52.670
After a series of Texas Chainsaw sequels that
got progressively more ludicrous, 2003's reboot

00:08:52.670 --> 00:08:56.710
looked to return the franchise to its original
form by having a group of unexpecting teens

00:08:56.710 --> 00:08:58.970
return to Leatherface's isolated abode.

00:08:58.970 --> 00:09:01.120
"What's wrong with you people?"

00:09:01.120 --> 00:09:03.710
"Oh my, my, my, my, my…"

00:09:03.710 --> 00:09:07.840
The pic benefited from modern cinematography
and sound effects, and one deliciously evil

00:09:07.840 --> 00:09:10.380
sheriff that made Leatherface seem like a
teddy bear.

00:09:10.380 --> 00:09:13.580
However, it added very little to the story
we already knew.

00:09:13.580 --> 00:09:17.380
Its sequel The Beginning, however, did some
digging as to why this cannibalistic family

00:09:17.380 --> 00:09:18.570
became what they were.

00:09:18.570 --> 00:09:22.100
The way their backstory developed was clever
and included some of the franchise's best

00:09:22.100 --> 00:09:23.100
dialogue.

00:09:23.100 --> 00:09:28.440
"No talking until after I've said grace, a------.”

00:09:28.440 --> 00:09:32.490
More importantly, the plot also paid more
attention to its societal environment—namely,

00:09:32.490 --> 00:09:36.140
the Vietnam War and the conscription that
plagued a generation's youth.

00:09:36.140 --> 00:09:38.840
"You kids okay?"

00:09:38.840 --> 00:09:42.550
It still didn't transcend the original by
any stretch of the imagination, but it certainly

00:09:42.550 --> 00:09:45.730
bested the previous sequels and reboots in
the series.

00:09:45.730 --> 00:09:48.090
"You blasphemous bitch!

00:09:48.090 --> 00:09:52.730
This is redemption, lady, that’s what this
is!”

00:09:52.730 --> 00:09:53.730
Thanks for watching!

00:09:53.730 --> 00:09:55.720
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:09:55.720 --> 00:09:58.060
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

